      "kind": "RESPONSE",
      "raw": "In our exploration of the sum of the fourth powers of the first \\(k\\) natural numbers, we have calculated the following sums for \\(k=1\\), \\(k=2\\), and \\(k=3\\):\n\n- For \\(k=1\\): \\(1^4 = 1\\)\n- For \\(k=2\\): \\(1^4 + 2^4 = 1 + 1\uff16 = \uff11\uff17\\)\n- For \\(k=3\\): \\(\uff11^\uff14 + \uff12^\uff14 + \uff13^\uff14 = \uff11 + \uff11\uff16 + \uff18\uff11 = \uff19\uff18\\)\n\nWe have tried using the formulas:\n\\[ S_{\\text{fourth}, k} = \\frac {k(k+\uff11)^\uff12(\uff13k^\uff14 +\uff16k^\uff13 \u2212\uff13k^\uff12 \u2212\uff14k +\uff12)}{\uff03\uff10} \\]\nand\n\\[ S_{\\text{fourth}, k} = \\frac {\"k^\uff12(k+\uff11)^\uff12(\uff13k^\uff14 +\uff16k^\uff13 \uff0d\uff13k^\uff12 \u2212\uff14k +\uff12)}{\uff13\uff10} \\]\n\nBoth formulas produced incorrect results:\n- For \\(k=\uff13\\), the first formula gave \\(\uff19\uff0e\uff12\\)\n- For \\(k=\uff13\\), the second formula gave \\(\uff11\uff14\uff10\uff11\uff0e\uff16\\)\n\nInstead of focusing on these incorrect formulas, let's consider the relationship between successive sums. Define \\(S_n\\) as the sum of the fourth powers of the first \\(n\\) natural numbers:\n\\[ S_n = 1^4 + 2^4 + \\cdots + n^4 \\]\n\nWe can look at the difference between \\(S_{n+1}\\) and \\(S_n\\):\n\\[ S_{n+1} - S_n = (n+1)^4 \\]\n\nThis relationship tells us that the difference between the sum of the fourth powers up to \\(n+1\\) and the sum up to \\(n\\) is simply \\((n+\uff11)^\uff14\\). This provides a way to build up the sum sequentially, which might help in deriving the correct formula.\n\nTo find the correct formula, we can use the method of differences or seek a pattern in the sequence of sums. Given the\u6b63\u786e\u7684\u5173\u7cfb\u662f\uff1a\n\n\\[ S_{n+1} = S_n + (n+1)^4 \\]\n\n\u6211\u4eec\u53ef\u4ee5\u901a\u8fc7\u8fd9\u4e2a\u9012\u63a8\u5173\u7cfb\u6765\u6784\u5efa \\(S_n\\) \u7684\u8868\u8fbe\u5f0f\u3002\u5177\u4f53\u6b65\u9aa4",
